
  [32m// Notification[39m
  { [36mid[39m: uuid, [36mtaskId[39m: uuid, [36mkind[39m: [31m'due-soon'[39m | [31m'overdue'[39m,
    [36mthresholdAt[39m: [34mISO8601[39m, [36mcreatedAt[39m: [34mISO8601[39m, [36mreadAt[39m: [34mISO8601[39m | [34mnull[39m }

  Three things worth flagging, because they're where this design could go wrong:

  [1mThe fired-set is derived, not stored separately.[0m "Have I already notified about this task's [38;5;153mdue-soon[39m?" is answered by
  querying existing notifications for [38;5;153m(taskId, kind, thresholdAt)[39m. A separate fired-set would be a second source of
  truth that can drift from the feed — classic bug. The cost is that clearing a notification could re-fire it; we
  prevent that by keeping dismissed notifications with a [38;5;153mreadAt[39m, not deleting them.

  [1m[38;5;153mthresholdAt[39m is part of the identity.[0m If you push a task's due date back, that's a genuinely new threshold and [3mshould[0m
  be able to notify again. Keying on [38;5;153m(taskId, kind)[39m alone would silently suppress the second warning — which is the
  failure mode where someone misses a deadline because the app already told them about the old one.

  [1mDeleted tasks keep their log events.[0m The log is an audit trail; deleting a task removes it from the store and emits
  [38;5;153mtask.deleted[39m, but history survives. The activity view therefore has to render events for tasks that no longer exist —
  it shows the title captured in the event rather than looking it up.

  One decision left in this section, and it's the one that determines how noisy the feed feels day to day.
